      "writtenOn": "2022-05-10T05:48:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This may not always be the case - there is nothing stopping external functions from setting these signals, and it may be quite OK depending on the state of things.\nI don\u0027t think it is up to this function to assert just who should call it.\nThat doesn\u0027t mean it is correct for it to be called at any time, but I think this should be determined by the power sequence API contract or design.\n\nThe real issue here is the use of ap_power_force_shutdown(), which does bypass\nall the power sequence code and summarily force these signals.\nThe problem lies in that the power sequence code then has no co-ordination\nwith this action, and of course is just reacting to the collateral effect of the signals changing.\n\nI don\u0027t think the answer is to assert everything is to happen as part of the power sequence thread, but to figure out how to properly deal with this external request.\nIt may mean that ap_power_force_shutdown() as an external request needs to take a lock and then control the signals (setting a flag indicating it is a forced shutdown, so the power sequence thread can process it accordingly).\nOr it sends a request to the power sequence code to do the shutdown, though the idea of this forced shutdown is to act immediately (whatever that means).",
      "revId": "1a1f88280547edeee287f34571c42bd4f4683288",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
